Q: C++26 constant_wrapper and constant_arg_t A: <p>The issue is that there is a potential ambiguity between the tag treatment and <code>std::constant_wrapper</code>, in a rather contrived scenario.</p>
<p><code>std::constant_wrapper</code> has a call operator, and the semantics of <code>std::cw&lt;F&gt;(X)</code> are to yield <code>std::cw&lt;F(decltype(X)::value)&gt;</code>. It might change to conditionally just do <code>F(X)</code> as as well (see <a href="https://isocpp.org/files/papers/P3978R2.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">P3987R2</a>), but let's stick with the wrapped version for now (and the existence of the unwrapping call operator doesn't affect this reasoning).</p>
<p>What if you have an <code>F</code> and an <code>X</code> that potentially behave differently based on wrapping behavior?</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// Simply check if there is a V::value
inline constexpr F = []&lt;class V&gt;(V v) -&gt; bool {
    return requires { V::value; };
};

struct Answer { static constexpr int value = 42; };
inline constexpr Answer answer;
</code></pre>
<p>Now, <code>std::cw&lt;F&gt;(answer)</code> is <code>std::cw&lt;F(Answer::value)&gt;</code> is <code>std::cw&lt;false&gt;</code>. We unwrap on the way into the call, so since we invoke <code>F</code> with just an <code>int</code>, that's <code>false</code> — but it's a wrapped <code>false</code>.</p>
<p>But what is <code>std::function_ref&lt;bool(Answer)&gt;(std::cw&lt;F&gt;)(answer)</code>? Is this:</p>
<ul>
<li>treating <code>cw&lt;F&gt;</code> as a tag that we're initializing the <code>funcion_ref</code> with <code>F</code> in a non-dangling way? In this case, we're treating <code>F</code> as the callable, and <code>F(answer)</code> is <code>true</code>.</li>
<li>treating <code>cw&lt;F&gt;</code> as a callable that satisfies the signature <code>bool(Answer)</code>? In this case, we're treating <code>cw&lt;F&gt;</code> as the callable, and <code>cw&lt;F&gt;(answer)</code> is <code>cw&lt;false&gt;</code>, which converts to <code>false</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>We got different answers depending on our interpretation.</p>
<p>Note that other call wrappers don't have this tagged treatment, so <code>std::function&lt;bool(Answer)&gt;(cw&lt;F&gt;)(answer)</code> is <code>false</code>. So <code>function_ref&lt;Sig&gt;(cw&lt;F&gt;)</code> could behave differently from all other call wrappers (<code>function</code>, <code>copyable_function</code>, <code>move_only_function</code>).</p>
<p>But this really only happens when you have some callable such that <code>f(x)</code> and <code>f(cw&lt;x&gt;)</code> are both valid and have different behavior.</p>
<p>With <code>constant_arg</code>, there's only one possible treatment, so there's no ambiguity in this sense.</p>

Q: Spring Boot 3.3 migration. Cannot resolve reference to bean jpaSharedEM_entityManagerFactory A: <p>For information on using non-default Datasource settings, please refer to <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/how-to/data-access.html#howto.data-access.configure-two-datasources" rel="nofollow noreferrer">howto.data-access.configure-two-datasources</a></p>
<p>This is a proof-of-concept test.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>application.yaml</code> : remove <code>hibernate: hbm2ddl:</code></li>
<li><code>AnagSourceConfiguration.java</code> : The main changes are all in this file.</li>
